<html>
<title>Clock Speed</title>
<body>

<h1>Clock Speed</h1>

<p>
The Clock function lets you specify the clock speed of the CPU for the emulated computer in MHz.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/clock_speed.png" border="0" width="117" height="30">
</p>

<p>
The specified default values are the actual speeds of the original computers.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Values that are too high will not speed up the emulator, as the host computer will not be able to sustain the emulation at that speed.
</p>

<p>
When using the COMX-35, Cidelsa, Telmac TMC-600 or PECOM 64 with sound, a clock frequency change will also change the sound frequency. This is because the original computer derived the sound frequency from the video chip and the CDP1802 processor speed. Increasing the speed raises all sound frequencies.
</p>

<p>
The emulated video frequency stays the same regardless of the chosen CPU frequency, except when using the CDP1861 or CDP1864. High CPU speeds may cause the video frequency to change.
</p>

<p>
The effective CPU clock speed and vertical video frequency can be verified on the <a href="ProfilerMain.htm"><strong>Profiler</strong></a> tab.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> The values shown are calculated from the current emulator speed and may differ from the specified clock speed value.
</p>

<p>
Default frequencies as used in Emma 02:
</p>

